 For `eventsGroup`, `last` and `live` target the last group of events in the loaded transcript, not the last entry of the transcript overall. The `live` class and `live events` label are shown only for the most recent group of an active session, independent of the chosen mode. Manual collapsing/expanding is remembered per group in any mode. Because this release also changes the storage key events groups use to persist their disclosure state, every previously saved group disclosure choice resets once after upgrading.
 
+`ui.eventsGroupLimit` caps how many events (each thinking block, each tool call together with its result, or another technical entry — the same unit the group's `N events` summary counts) a single events group card can hold before it is cut into consecutive chunks, each its own events group card. It accepts an integer from 0 to 10000; the default is 111, and `0` disables splitting and reproduces the pre-existing single-card behavior. Every chunk of a series split into more than one chunk, including the first, carries a `part N/M` prefix before its summary, for example `part 2/4 · 111 events · 60 assistant · 51 tool`; an unsplit group keeps the plain summary as before. Chunks are governed by `ui.disclosure.eventsGroup` the same way ordinary groups are (only the last chunk is open in `live`/`last`, every chunk is open in `all`), and manual expanding/collapsing is remembered per chunk. Splitting is purely client-side and deterministic from the loaded messages, so a long-running session and its reloaded history always split at the same boundaries. In `live`/`last` mode, a chunk that opened by default as the last chunk of its series and then loses that status because the series continues into a new chunk stays open ("sticky") while the user is scrolled away from the bottom, instead of collapsing immediately; scrolling back to the bottom, or using the scroll-to-latest button, collapses every sticky chunk at once.
